Z||000000||Elvira||07-08-2001||01:06 PM||||Well I have searched the archives and can't seem to find info on this subject.  I recently got a Blue 87GT which someone had repainted a different blue and did not do ANY prepping before painting (even painted over dirt by the moulding).  I was wondering if anyone had successfully removed repaint without removing original paint and if so, HOW??  I want to restore the color to the original blue without having to totally strip her down (that is going to be my fall/winter project).  If it cn't be removed like that then whatt is the best way to bring out a shine on a VERY neglected car.  I have tried rubbing compound, polishing compound, and it has helped some, but I know she can shine better!  Thanks in advance!  <BR><P>------------------<BR>Elvira<BR>Blue 87GT<BR>Black 86GT 5sp x 2<BR>86SC t-top 58k miles||||reg||5
Z||000001||Mach10||07-08-2001||02:18 PM||||The only thing I could think of would be to break out the fine-grit sandpaper, and strip it down to the base coat, then get it re-clearcoated. <BR>Honestly? Strip it down to the panel, get it totally redone. The solvents from that newer paint will have eaten into and bit into the older coat. Get it redone, it'll look better. Hey, at least there's not body rust, eh?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||9
Z||000002||KRMFiero||07-08-2001||02:36 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Mach10:<BR><B>Hey, at least there's not body rust, eh?   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Hahah that reminds me of when i first bought my 85 and my dad asked how come the snow wasnt melting of the (front) hood lol<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||rogergarrison||07-08-2001||07:16 PM||||if you do ANY sanding, its gonna have to be base color coated and cleared. Clearing over old sanded basecoat is only a temporary fix. Also if its a really poor job, ive had good luck at a power car wash stripping all the bad paint off. find a spot peeled or chipped, put the wand rite on it and go in long strokes and watch the paint fly. Costs about $10 worth of quarters. I stripped a bad factory paint job off a Taurus from bumper to bumper in about 30 minutes.<p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 07-08-2001).]||||reg||1
